<!-- MODULE 5: OPENING NARRATIVE -->
<section class="cs-exec">
  <div class="cs-exec-inner">
    <p class="cs-exec-body">Skincare brands that have built their customer base through Meta typically arrive at a Google Ads launch with more assets than they realize. Two or three years of paid social means a purchaser list, tested product knowledge, a record of what converts and at what price point, and customers who know the brand well enough to search for it by name. The launch is not starting from zero. It is extending into a different type of demand.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body" style="margin-top:18px;">Google captures buyers who express intent explicitly through search. That is a different behavioral context from Meta, where demand is created through reach and creative. The strategic question for a brand in this position is not simply how to turn on Google Ads. It is how to apply what the brand already knows to an environment that works differently.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body" style="margin-top:18px;">This brand had spent two years building its customer base through Meta. Google Ads was the next channel they wanted to test. Their brief was simple: <em>"Just set up Google Ads and see how it goes."</em></p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body" style="margin-top:18px;">That brief told us two things: there was real opportunity here, and the default approach would spend the first few months learning things we could establish before launch.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body" style="margin-top:18px;">Rather than launch broadly and reorganize the account later, we used the pre-launch period to review conversion measurement, assess the product feed, prepare the brand's existing purchaser data for Customer Match, and build the initial keyword set. The account launched with two campaign types: Search and Performance Max.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body" style="margin-top:18px;">The account recorded a positive platform-reported return early in the launch period. At approximately 60 days, it recorded 4.91&times; blended ROAS, if independently documented. Across the complete January&ndash;May 2026 reporting period, Google Ads recorded $34,295.86 in conversion value from $8,949.03 in spend, a 3.83&times; blended ROAS.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body" style="margin-top:18px;">By the end of month five, the account had five months of its own search-term, product, and conversion history to inform the next stage of testing.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- MODULE 6: WHAT WE PREPARED -->
<section class="sk-prep-section" aria-labelledby="prep-heading">
  <div class="sk-prep-inner">
    <div class="sk-prep-header">
      <span class="cs-eyebrow">Pre-Launch Work</span>
      <h2 class="cs-h2" id="prep-heading" style="margin-bottom:12px;">What We Prepared Before the First Dollar Was Spent</h2>
      <p class="cs-para" style="font-size:15px;margin-bottom:0;">The account's initial structure was decided before spend began. Four areas were reviewed and prepared in advance.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="sk-prep-list" role="list">

      <div class="sk-prep-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="sk-prep-num" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
        <div>
          <span class="sk-prep-area">Product Data</span>
          <div class="sk-prep-title">Product Feed Reviewed and Revised</div>
          <p class="sk-prep-body">The existing product titles reflected the brand's internal naming conventions, useful for existing customers but less aligned with how a new shopper might search. We revised priority products to surface clearer attributes: product type, key ingredient, format, and size where relevant. A real example of the shift: from brand-led naming like <em>[Brand] Brightening Complex &middot; Luminance Series</em> toward search-oriented language like <em>Vitamin C Brightening Serum &middot; 30mL</em>. The intent was to give Shopping and Performance Max stronger product information at launch rather than rely on campaign optimization to compensate later.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sk-prep-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="sk-prep-num" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
        <div>
          <span class="sk-prep-area">Initial Campaign Scope</span>
          <div class="sk-prep-title">Campaign Structure Planned Against Available Budget</div>
          <p class="sk-prep-body">The brand was open to launching several campaign types at once. Given the available budget and the absence of any existing Google account history, we recommended a narrower initial structure. Search provided direct control over the initial keyword set. Performance Max provided broader feed-led coverage with the brand's customer data available as an audience input. The goal was to give each campaign enough budget and conversion opportunity to generate useful data before adding complexity.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sk-prep-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="sk-prep-num" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
        <div>
          <span class="sk-prep-area">First-Party Customer Data</span>
          <div class="sk-prep-title">Existing Purchaser Data Prepared for Customer Match</div>
          <p class="sk-prep-body">The brand already had verified purchaser data from its Meta-led growth period. We prepared that data for Customer Match and made it available as an audience input before the account went live. This gave Google an additional first-party signal at launch. It did not eliminate exploration, guarantee faster learning, or restrict Performance Max to users who resembled the uploaded list. Audience signals inform where Google looks first. They do not constrain where it looks.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sk-prep-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="sk-prep-num" aria-hidden="true">04</span>
        <div>
          <span class="sk-prep-area">Conversion Measurement</span>
          <div class="sk-prep-title">Purchase Tracking and Attribution Settings Reviewed</div>
          <p class="sk-prep-body">We verified the purchase conversion action, conversion-value reporting, and attribution settings before the account went live. Meta and Google were not treated as directly additive or perfectly comparable. Their attribution models, identity resolution, and reporting logic differ in meaningful ways. We documented those differences before launch so early decisions would not rely on a false one-to-one comparison between platform-reported results.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>

