<!-- MODULE 9: FIVE LAUNCH DECISIONS -->
<section class="cs-interventions" aria-labelledby="decisions-heading">
  <div class="cs-interventions-inner">
    <h2 class="cs-h2" id="decisions-heading">Five Launch Decisions We Made Before Going Live</h2>
    <p class="cs-para" style="font-size:15px;margin-top:-14px;margin-bottom:32px;">The account's initial structure was decided before spend began, not rebuilt after the budget had already been distributed.</p>

    <div class="cs-intervention-list">

      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">01</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Prepared the Product Feed Before Launch</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">The existing product titles leaned heavily on brand and collection language. We revised priority products to surface clearer attributes: product type, key ingredient, format, and size. The objective was to give Shopping and Performance Max stronger product information from the first impression, rather than relying on campaign optimization to compensate for unclear feed data later.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">02</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Kept the Initial Campaign Scope Focused</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">The account launched with Search and Performance Max rather than distributing the available budget across several campaign types immediately. The goal was not to minimize campaign count for its own sake. It was to give each initial campaign enough budget and conversion opportunity to generate useful account-specific data before adding complexity.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">03</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Made Existing Purchaser Data Available Through Customer Match</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">The brand already had verified purchaser data from its Meta-led growth period. We prepared and uploaded the eligible customer list before launch and used it as an audience input where supported. Customer Match gave Google additional first-party information at launch. It did not eliminate exploration, guarantee a specific learning timeline, or restrict Performance Max to users who resembled the uploaded customers.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">04</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Started Search With Phrase and Exact Match</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">We began with a defined phrase- and exact-match keyword set to maintain tighter control over the initial search-term mix. Broad match was not excluded on principle. It was deferred until the account had enough conversion history and evidence to evaluate whether broader query coverage would improve incremental volume without reducing efficiency beyond the brand's acceptable range.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">05</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Reviewed Measurement Before Launch</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">We verified the purchase conversion action, conversion-value reporting, and attribution settings before the account went live. Meta and Google were not treated as directly comparable platforms. Their attribution models and identity resolution differ in ways that matter when interpreting early results. We documented those differences before launch so early bid decisions would not rely on a false one-to-one comparison between platform-reported numbers.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>

